                return true
            choices[i] <- 0
        return false

Why it works

Each star has three options. The choices array starts with all zeros.
Advancing the array like a base-three counter visits every combination,
including the one empty combination when there are zero stars. For each
combination, a closing parenthesis pops an earlier unmatched opening.
An empty stack at the end means that all openings were matched. The
program accepts exactly when some combination passes that check.

Validator complexity

Let n be the number of symbols before '$' and w the number of stars.
There are 3^w combinations. With O(1) amortized pushes, clearing the
stack, checking a combination, and updating the choices take
O(n + 1) amortized time per attempt.
The worst-case time bound is O((n + 1) * 3^w). The choices array uses
O(w) space and the stack can reach O(n) space, giving O(n + 1)
auxiliary space.

